  <header class="post-header"><div class="post-header-inner">
    <a href="/blog/" class="post-breadcrumb">Back to Blog</a>
    <h1>How Much Does a Website Cost in 2026? 5 Factors</h1>
    <div class="post-meta"><span>By <a href="/about/"><strong>Kevin Freeland</strong></a></span><span>March 14, 2026</span><span>5 min read</span></div>
  </div></header>
  <div class="post-body">
    <div class="post-featured-img"><img src="/blog/images/how-much-does-a-website-cost.jpg" alt="Planning a business website project" /></div>
    <p>What a website costs in 2026 depends on five things: scope, the number of page types, integrations, content and maintenance. A simple site with one layout and a contact form is a much smaller job than a store with product pages, checkout, email signups and order updates. Pin those five down and you can compare quotes fairly.</p>
    <p>A useful quote starts with the work the site needs to do. A service business that needs phone calls has different requirements from a store that needs product pages and checkout. Before comparing proposals, write down what a visitor should be able to do and what your team needs to manage.</p>
    <p>Define these items in writing before you request a quote:</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Factor</th><th>Define before requesting a quote</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Scope</td><td>What the site must do at launch, what can wait, and which devices, forms and buying paths must pass testing</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Page types</td><td>Which pages share a layout and which need different features</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Integrations</td><td>What data moves and how failures are handled</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Content</td><td>Who writes, supplies, enters and approves it</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Maintenance</td><td>Who controls accounts, updates the site and handles changes after launch</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>How does scope affect website cost?</h2>
    <p>Describe the problem the website needs to solve. That might be helping customers understand your services, improving a confusing checkout or making it easier to book an appointment. Name the primary action and the steps that lead to it.</p>
    <p>Separate what must work at launch from what can wait. Put those decisions in writing so everyone can tell when a new request changes the project. A clear scope also makes it easier to agree on what finished means.</p>

    <h2>Count the page types, not just the pages</h2>
    <p>A set of service pages that shares a layout is different from a collection of pages with distinct designs and features. List the pages you need, then group the ones that can use the same structure.</p>
    <p>Include the pages people often forget: contact forms, confirmation screens, search results, policy pages and error pages. If you are replacing an existing site, include redirects and the content that needs to move across.</p>
    <p>Ask who will enter the content and check it. Building a template does not necessarily include preparing every page that uses it.</p>

    <h2>Define what needs to connect</h2>
    <p>Booking tools, payment systems, email platforms and customer records can add useful features. They also add setup and testing work. For each connection, describe what information moves, where it goes and what should happen if the connection fails.</p>
    <p>A form is a good example. Seeing a success message is only part of the job. The inquiry also needs to reach the right person, keep the necessary context and trigger any agreed follow-up. Include that whole path in the scope.</p>
    <p>For a store, confirm the requirements for products, payments, inventory, shipping and order notifications. Check which features the existing platform supports before commissioning custom work.</p>

    <h2>Decide who supplies the content</h2>
    <p>Copy, photography and product information take time to prepare. Agree on who writes each page, who supplies the images and who approves the finished content. If existing material needs editing or replacing, include that work from the start.</p>
    <p>Write for the questions a customer actually asks. Explain the offer, show relevant evidence and make the next step clear. Search planning should support those pages, including their titles, descriptions and internal links.</p>
    <p>Check image permissions and make sure claims have support. A polished layout cannot fix unclear information or a claim the business cannot substantiate.</p>

    <h2>Include testing and handoff</h2>
    <p>Agree on how the site will be checked before launch. That includes phone layouts, keyboard access, page speed, forms, checkout and any integrations. Define who fixes issues found during review.</p>
    <p>Ask what you will receive at handoff: account access, instructions, source files where applicable and a clear explanation of how to change routine content. Your team should know who controls the domain, hosting and connected services.</p>

    <h2>Know who maintains it</h2>
    <p>The work continues after launch. Someone needs to handle software updates, backups, broken connections and content changes. Name that owner and agree on the support process before the project starts.</p>
    <p>List recurring services such as domain registration, hosting, platform subscriptions and licensed tools. Check renewal terms, usage limits and what happens if you change providers. Separate ongoing services from the initial build in the proposal.</p>
    <p>Choose a setup your team can maintain. If a simple change always requires outside help, include that dependency in your decision.</p>

    <h2>Compare proposals against the same plan</h2>
    <p>Send each provider the same brief. Ask them to explain what is included, what you need to supply and which assumptions could change the scope. Look for a clear review process and a specific handoff.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Which pages and layouts are included?</li>
      <li>Who writes and enters the content?</li>
      <li>Which integrations will be built and tested?</li>
      <li>How are revisions and new requests handled?</li>
      <li>Who maintains the site and responds when something breaks?</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Relevant examples help too. Look at live sites, try them on your phone and follow the main customer action. Ask who on their team will do the work. To see what we include in a build, visit our <a href="/services/website-design/">website design service</a>. For the pages and customer actions a local business site needs, read our <a href="/blog/website-design-florida-small-business/">website design guide</a>.</p>

    <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <h3>How much does a website cost for a small business?</h3>
    <p>It depends on five things: scope, page types, integrations, who supplies the content and who maintains the site after launch. Give each provider the same brief, including the site's main action and the launch checks. Ask them to separate the initial build from ongoing support, so you compare the same work.</p>
    <h3>Why do some web design agencies charge more than others?</h3>
    <p>Website quotes can cover different amounts of research, design, writing, content entry, integration work and testing. Ask each provider to identify what is included and excluded. Compare the same requirements, review process and handoff before deciding whether a higher quote buys work your business actually needs.</p>
    <h3>What ongoing costs should I budget for after my website launches?</h3>
    <p>Plan for domain renewal, hosting or platform subscriptions, software updates, backups and content changes. Include support for forms, checkout and connected tools. Ask who owns each task, what the service covers and how you can move to another provider if the arrangement no longer fits.</p>

